{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 cv_bridge: The OpenCV-ROS Bridge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 OpenCV (the computer vision library) and ROS use different image formats. cv_bridge translates between them.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Why We Need It:}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab OpenCV uses numpy arrays (simple, fast)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab ROS uses sensor_msgs/Image (structured, network-ready)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab cv_bridge converts between them efficiently\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Basic Usage:}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 from cv_bridge import CvBridge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 bridge = CvBridge()\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 # OpenCV to ROS\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 opencv_image = cv2.imread('photo.jpg')\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 ros_msg = bridge.cv2_to_imgmsg(opencv_image, encoding='bgr8')\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 # ROS to OpenCV\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 ros_msg = Image() # received from topic\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 opencv_image = bridge.imgmsg_to_cv2(ros_msg, 'bgr8')\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 2. Understanding ROS 2 Node Architecture\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 2.1 The Lifecycle of a ROS 2 Node\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Every ROS 2 node follows a specific lifecycle from initialization to shutdown. Understanding this flow is essential for proper node development.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Main Function Structure:}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 import rclpy\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 from rclpy.node import Node\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 def main():\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 # Step 1: Initialize ROS 2\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 rclpy.init()\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 # Step 2: Create node instance\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 node = CameraNode()\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 # Step 3: Keep node alive (event loop)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 rclpy.spin(node)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 # Step 4: Cleanup\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 node.destroy_node()\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 rclpy.shutdown()\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Let me explain each step in detail:}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Step 1 - rclpy.init():}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Sets up ROS 2 communication infrastructure\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Connects to the ROS 2 network (DDS layer)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Must be called before creating any nodes\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Step 2 - CameraNode():}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Creates instance of your node class\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Calls __init__() which sets up publishers, timers, hardware\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Node is now ready to start working\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Step 3 - rclpy.spin(node):}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Enters the event loop (infinite loop)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Executes timer callbacks at their scheduled times\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Processes incoming messages\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Keeps running until Ctrl+C is pressed\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Step 4 - Cleanup:}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab destroy_node() calls your cleanup code\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Closes files, stops hardware, releases resources\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab rclpy.shutdown() disconnects from ROS network\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 2.2 Node Initialization Pattern\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The __init__() method is where you set up everything your node needs. There is a standard pattern to follow.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Standard Initialization Pattern:}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 class CameraNode(Node):\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 def __init__(self):\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 # STEP 1: Call parent class (REQUIRED!)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 super().__init__('camera_node')\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 # STEP 2: Declare parameters\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 self.declare_parameter('width', 640)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 self.declare_parameter('height', 480)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 self.declare_parameter('fps', 30)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 # STEP 3: Get parameter values\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 width = self.get_parameter('width').value\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 height = self.get_parameter('height').value\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 # STEP 4: Create publishers\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 self.publisher_ = self.create_publisher(\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Image, 'camera/image_raw', 10)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 # STEP 5: Initialize utilities\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 self.bridge = CvBridge()\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 self.buffer = b''\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 # STEP 6: Initialize hardware\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 self.process = self.start_camera(width, height)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 # STEP 7: Create timer (starts callbacks)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 self.timer = self.create_timer(0.001, self.timer_callback)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 # STEP 8: Log success\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 self.get_logger().info('Camera node started')\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 2.3 Timer Callbacks: The Heart of Your Node\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Timer callbacks are how your node does work repeatedly. Understanding timers is crucial for camera nodes because they need to continuously capture and publish frames.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Creating a Timer:}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 # Create timer that runs at 30 Hz (30 times per second)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 self.timer = self.create_timer(1.0/30.0, self.timer_callback)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 # ^^^^^^^^ ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 # period function to call\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Why Use Timers Instead of While Loops?}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Bad Approach (blocking):}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 def main():\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 node = CameraNode()\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 while True: # BAD! Blocks everything!\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 node.capture_and_publish()\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 # ROS can't process other things!\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Good Approach (non-blocking):}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 def __init__(self):\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 self.timer = self.create_timer(0.033, self.capture_and_publish)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 # ROS manages the loop, callback is non-blocking\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Benefits of Timers:}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Non-blocking: ROS can still process other tasks\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Precise timing: ROS ensures callbacks run on schedule\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Clean shutdown: Ctrl+C works properly\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Multiple timers: Can have different rates for different tasks\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 3. Part 1: Creating the IMX708 Camera Node\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 3.1 The Problem and Our Solution\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The IMX708 camera uses libcamera, which is not directly compatible with standard ROS camera drivers. We need a bridge between libcamera and ROS 2.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b The Challenge:}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab IMX708 uses CSI interface (not USB)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Requires libcamera (modern camera framework)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab ROS camera drivers expect V4L2 or direct API access\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Direct libcamera integration is complex\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Our Solution: rpicam-vid Bridge}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Use rpicam-vid (command-line tool)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Run it as subprocess from Python\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab rpicam-vid outputs MJPEG stream to stdout\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Read stream, decode frames, publish to ROS\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Architecture Diagram:}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Hardware Level:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 [IMX708 Camera] --> CSI Interface --> [Raspberry Pi 5]\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Software Level:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 [libcamera] handles camera communication\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 |\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 [rpicam-vid subprocess] encodes as MJPEG\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 |\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 [stdout pipe] streams MJPEG data\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 |\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 [Python ROS Node] reads, decodes, publishes\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 |\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 [/camera/image_raw topic]\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 3.2 Understanding MJPEG Streaming\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 MJPEG (Motion JPEG) is a video format where each frame is a complete JPEG image. Understanding how to parse this stream is key to our implementation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b What is MJPEG?}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab MJPEG = Series of JPEG images sent one after another\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Each frame is independently encoded (no inter-frame compression)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Easy to parse because each JPEG has clear start/end markers\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b JPEG Boundary Markers:}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Start marker: 0xFF 0xD8 (two bytes)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 End marker: 0xFF 0xD9 (two bytes)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 MJPEG stream looks like:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 [0xFF 0xD8...JPEG data...0xFF 0xD9][0xFF 0xD8...JPEG data...0xFF 0xD9]...\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 ^ ^ ^ ^\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Start End Start End\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Parsing Algorithm:}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 buffer = b'' # Empty buffer\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 while True:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 # Read data from subprocess\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 chunk = process.stdout.read(4096)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 buffer += chunk # Accumulate data\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 # Find JPEG boundaries\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 start = buffer.find(b'\\xff\\xd8')\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 end = buffer.find(b'\\xff\\xd9')\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 if start != -1 and end != -1 and end > start:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 # We have complete JPEG!\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 jpg = buffer[start:end+2] # Extract (+2 for end marker)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 buffer = buffer[end+2:] # Remove from buffer\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 # Decode and use\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 frame = cv2.imdecode(np.frombuffer(jpg, np.uint8), cv2.IMREAD_COLOR)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 3.3 Complete IMX708 Node Implementation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Now we put it all together. This is the complete, production-ready implementation with detailed comments explaining each part.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b File: imx708_publisher.py}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 #!/usr/bin/env python3\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 """\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 IMX708 Camera Publisher Node for ROS 2\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Publishes camera feed from Raspberry Pi Camera Module 3\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 """\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 import rclpy\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 from rclpy.node import Node\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 from sensor_msgs.msg import Image\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 from cv_bridge import CvBridge\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 import cv2\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 import subprocess\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 import numpy as np\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 class IMX708Publisher(Node):\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 """Node that publishes images from IMX708 camera"""\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 def __init__(self):\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 # Initialize parent Node class\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 super().__init__('imx708_camera_node')\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 # Declare configurable parameters\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 self.declare_parameter('width', 640)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 self.declare_parameter('height', 480)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 self.declare_parameter('fps', 30)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 self.declare_parameter('camera_id', 0)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Due to space constraints in this document, the complete code is available in your workspace at: ~/Documents/Eon/src/camera/camera/imx708_publisher.py}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 3.4 Key Concepts in the IMX708 Implementation\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Let's break down the critical concepts that make this node work.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Concept 1: Subprocess Management}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab subprocess.Popen() creates a new process running rpicam-vid\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab stdout=subprocess.PIPE captures the output\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab We read from process.stdout like a file\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Must terminate the process on shutdown\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Concept 2: Buffer Management}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Data arrives in chunks (4096 bytes at a time)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab One JPEG frame might span multiple chunks\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Buffer accumulates data until complete frame found\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab After extracting frame, remove it from buffer\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Concept 3: Image Encoding}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab cv2.imdecode() converts JPEG bytes to numpy array\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab cv_bridge.cv2_to_imgmsg() converts numpy to ROS message\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab encoding='bgr8' means 8-bit BGR color (OpenCV default)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab header.stamp records capture time\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab header.frame_id identifies which camera\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Concept 4: Timer Rate Selection}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Timer at 1ms (0.001s) checks for new data frequently\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Actual publish rate depends on camera FPS\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Fast timer = low latency, but uses more CPU\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Could use 10ms or 33ms for lower CPU usage\sa180\par}
