{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 4.2 ToF Camera Architecture\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The ToF node uses the official Arducam SDK, which provides direct API access. This is simpler than the rpicam-vid bridge but requires proper hardware access.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Data Flow:}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 [ToF Camera Hardware]\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 |\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 CSI + I2C interfaces\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 |\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 [ArducamDepthCamera SDK]\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 |\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Direct API calls\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 |\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 [ROS 2 Node]\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 |\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Publishes to:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 |\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 - /point_cloud (3D points)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 - /depth_frame (depth array)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Key Differences from IMX708 Approach:}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab No subprocess - direct Python API\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab No MJPEG parsing - SDK returns numpy arrays\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Simpler but requires specific device index (8)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Must handle I2C permissions\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 4.3 Understanding Point Clouds\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A point cloud is a collection of 3D points (x, y, z coordinates) representing the physical world. The ToF camera measures depth and converts it to 3D points.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b How ToF Measures Depth:}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab ToF = Time of Flight\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Camera emits infrared light\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Light bounces off objects and returns\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Measures time for light to return\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Calculates distance: depth = (speed of light \u215 ? time) / 2\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Converting Depth to 3D Points:}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 For each pixel at position (u, v):\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 1. Get depth value z (in meters)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 2. Calculate x = (u - cx) \u215 ? z / fx\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 3. Calculate y = (v - cy) \u215 ? z / fy\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Where:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 cx, cy = camera center (image width/2, height/2)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 fx, fy = focal lengths (from camera intrinsics)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Confidence Values:}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Each depth measurement has confidence (0-255)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Low confidence = unreliable measurement\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Filter out points with confidence < 30\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Results in cleaner point clouds\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 4.4 ToF Node Implementation Concepts\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The ToF node uses the official Arducam package. Here are the key concepts you need to understand.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Concept 1: Device Index Issue}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 When running both cameras simultaneously, the ToF camera is at device index 8 (not 0). This is because:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Both cameras use the CSI interface\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab They are enumerated by the system in order\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab IMX708 takes lower indices\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab ToF ends up at index 8\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Concept 2: I2C Permissions}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The ToF camera uses I2C for communication, which requires special permissions:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Create i2c group: sudo groupadd i2c\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Add user to group: sudo usermod -a -G i2c $USER\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Create udev rule for /dev/i2c-* devices\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Reboot for changes to take effect\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Concept 3: Threading for Point Cloud Generation}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The official Arducam package uses threading to separate data acquisition from publishing:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Background thread requests frames continuously\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Converts depth to 3D points\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Main thread (timer) publishes at 30 Hz\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Prevents blocking if computation is slow\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Concept 4: Multiple Topic Publishing}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The ToF node publishes two complementary topics:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab /point_cloud - For visualization in RViz, SLAM\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab /depth_frame - Raw depth for custom processing\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Both synchronized (same timestamp)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Choose based on your application needs\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 5. Advanced Concepts and Data Flow\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 5.1 Complete System Data Flow\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Now that you understand both nodes, let's see how data flows through the entire system from hardware to ROS topics.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Hardware Level:}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 [IMX708 Camera] --CSI--> [Raspberry Pi 5 RP1 Chip]\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 [ToF Camera] --CSI+I2C--> [Raspberry Pi 5 RP1 Chip]\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Driver Level:}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 [RP1 Chip] --> [libcamera (IMX708)]\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 --> [Arducam SDK (ToF)]\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Application Level:}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 [libcamera] --> [rpicam-vid] --> [IMX708 Node]\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 [Arducam SDK] ----------------> [ToF Node]\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b ROS Level:}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 [IMX708 Node] --> /camera/image_raw topic\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 [ToF Node] ----> /point_cloud topic\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 --> /depth_frame topic\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 5.2 Timing and Synchronization\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Understanding timing is crucial for robotics applications where sensor data needs to be synchronized.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Frame Timestamps:}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Each message has header.stamp (ROS timestamp)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Set with self.get_clock().now().to_msg()\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Records when frame was captured\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Critical for sensor fusion and SLAM\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Publishing Rates:}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab IMX708: Up to 120 fps (depending on resolution)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab ToF: 30 fps (fixed by hardware)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Both run independently (not synchronized)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Use message_filters for synchronization if needed\sa180\par}
